member.summary:
Decides, as a pure function, which request-type SLA rows are enforced.
Only the unambiguous subset is enforced; everything else falls back to the per-priority policy and says why (never a silent guess). A row is enforced only when ALL of these hold:
it is active;
is (the other triggers need event plumbing that does not exist);
is explicitly set (null = business vs. calendar days, weekend and holiday treatment all undecided);
every duration it uses is a single value — Target equal to Maximum, or only one of them set. A range (Target < Maximum) is NOT enforced: which bound is "target" and which is "breach" is undecided;
for a business-hours basis, an active calendar exists to count against.
Units. Minutes and Hours are what they say, counted on the row's clock (business minutes under BusinessHours, calendar minutes under TwentyFourSeven). Days under TwentyFourSeven is 24 h x n. Days under BusinessHours is n x the calendar's business-day length of business time — the same convention the per-priority policy uses for "3 business days" (SlaReferenceData.BusinessMinutesPerDay), walked by so non-working days and holidays are skipped.
Immediate. IsImmediate ("Immediately") yields a resolution due equal to the clock start. It is documented, deterministic, and means the deadline is already due: the next check flags it unless the ticket is resolved at that instant.
Pause flag. true/false is explicit configuration independent of how the durations are interpreted, so it is reported for every active row, even one whose durations are not enforced. null inherits the approved global rule. PausesOnPendingInternal is never read (undecided).
